    <comment>[description] Explanatory paragraph under the thumbnail-cache cleanup suggestion. 'Explorer' means the Windows file manager; a thumbnail is the small preview image shown for a picture or video. {0} is an already-formatted size.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_ThumbnailCache_Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Caché de miniaturas</value>
    <comment>[label] Name of the cleanup rule targeting Windows Explorer's thumbnail cache database files. Shown as the rule's label in the Cleanup page rule list. Title case, no trailing period.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_ThumbnailCache_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Caché de miniaturas ({0} archivos)</value>
    <comment>[title] Headline of the thumbnail-cache cleanup suggestion. {0} is a small integer count of thumbcache_*.db files found. The '(s)' covers singular and plural for an unknown count.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsErrorReporting_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Volcados de bloqueo e informes de diagnóstico que se generan cuando un programa o el propio Windows encuentra un error. Se pueden eliminar sin riesgo: Microsoft ya ha recibido los informes que se haya optado por enviar. Ahorro estimado: {0}.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanatory paragraph under the Windows Error Reporting cleanup suggestion. Its job is to reassure: deleting local copies does not cancel or withhold reports the user already opted to send. {0} is an already-formatted size.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsErrorReporting_Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informes de errores de Windows</value>
    <comment>[label] Name of the cleanup rule targeting Windows Error Reporting crash dumps and diagnostic reports. Shown as the rule's label in the Cleanup page rule list. Use the wording Windows itself uses for error reporting.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsErrorReporting_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informes de errores de Windows ({0} ubicaciones)</value>
    <comment>[title] Headline of the Windows Error Reporting cleanup suggestion. {0} is a small integer count of folders and dump files found across the system. The '(s)' covers singular and plural for an unknown count.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsUpdateCache_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Paquetes de actualización descargados que ya se han aplicado. Windows volverá a descargarlos solo si hay que aplicar de nuevo la misma actualización. Ahorro estimado: {0}.</value>
    <comment>[description] Explanatory paragraph under the Windows Update cache cleanup suggestion. Its job is to reassure: the updates themselves stay installed, only the downloaded installer packages go. {0} is an already-formatted size.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsUpdateCache_Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Caché de Windows Update</value>
    <comment>[label] Name of the cleanup rule targeting the Windows Update download cache under SoftwareDistribution\Download. Shown as the rule's label in the Cleanup page rule list. 'Windows Update' is the official feature name.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Rule_WindowsUpdateCache_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Caché de Windows Update ({0} archivos)</value>
    <comment>[title] Headline of the Windows Update cache cleanup suggestion. {0} is an already-grouped file count such as '24,918' — do not reformat it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_CannotBeUndone" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Esta acción no se puede deshacer.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_CleanUpSelected_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limpiar lo seleccionado…</value>
    <comment>[safety] The Cleanup page's execute button, at the bottom next to the selected total. Opens a confirmation dialog before anything is deleted; the trailing ellipsis signals that a dialog follows.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_CleanUp_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Limpiar</value>
    <comment>[safety] Confirm button of the real-cleanup confirmation dialog on the Cleanup page. Pressing it starts deleting the selected items.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ClearEntireDownloads_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Vaciar toda la carpeta Descargas (subopción)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label of the toggle switch on the Cleanup page that widens the Downloads cleanup from installer files only to the user's whole Downloads folder. Turning it on puts personal files at risk, so the wording must stay explicit about 'entire'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmDryRun_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Esta acción simulará la limpieza de {0} de elementos seleccionados sin eliminar nada. ¿Desea continuar?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the dry-run confirmation dialog on the Cleanup page. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. Must stay clear that this run deletes nothing.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmDryRun_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Confirmar la vista previa de simulación</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog shown when the user starts a cleanup with 'Dry run' ticked. A dry run only simulates; nothing is deleted.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmPermanentAfterPreview_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Esta acción eliminará definitivamente la selección exacta de {0} de la vista previa completada correctamente. Esta acción no se puede deshacer. Los archivos se volverán a validar y las rutas que hayan cambiado se omitirán por seguridad. ¿Desea continuar?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the last confirmation dialog before permanent deletion on the Cleanup page. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. 'Fail closed' means a file whose path changed since the preview is skipped rather than deleted. Never soften 'cannot be undone'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmPermanentAfterPreview_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Confirmar la eliminación definitiva</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the extra confirmation dialog shown when the user chooses 'Delete Permanently' from the dry-run result dialog. This is the last step before files are destroyed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmPermanent_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Esta acción eliminará DEFINITIVAMENTE {0} de archivos y carpetas seleccionados. Esta acción no se puede deshacer. El tamaño mostrado es el tamaño lógico de los archivos; el espacio en disco realmente recuperado puede diferir. ¿Desea continuar?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the confirmation dialog for a real cleanup when 'Send to Recycle Bin' is off, so the files are destroyed. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. PERMANENTLY is capitalised in English for emphasis; keep the same emphasis and never soften 'cannot be undone'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ConfirmRecycleBin_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Esta acción enviará {0} de archivos y carpetas seleccionados a la Papelera de reciclaje (recuperable). El espacio en disco seguirá ocupado hasta que se vacíe la Papelera de reciclaje. ¿Desea continuar?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the confirmation dialog for a real cleanup when 'Send to Recycle Bin' is on. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. The user must be able to tell this apart from the permanent-deletion message at a glance: this one is recoverable, and the disk space is not freed until the Recycle Bin is emptied.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Confirm_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Confirmar la limpieza</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog shown when the user starts a real cleanup (dry run not ticked). Files will actually be removed after this dialog.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_DeletePermanently_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar definitivamente</value>
    <comment>[safety] Button that runs the real cleanup with permanent, unrecoverable deletion. Used both as the secondary button of the cleanup result dialog (next to 'Delete (Recycle Bin)') and as the confirm button of the follow-up permanent-deletion dialog. Distinct from the existing Safety_PermanentDelete key, which is worded 'Permanently delete'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_DeleteRecycleBin_Button" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar (Papelera de reciclaje)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Primary button in the cleanup result dialog after a clean dry run. Runs the real cleanup with the recoverable Recycle Bin path. Sits directly next to the permanent-deletion button, so the two must be impossible to confuse.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Options_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mantenga desactivadas las categorías de riesgo salvo que comprenda sus efectos secundarios. La simulación sigue disponible antes de cualquier eliminación.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper text under the 'Cleanup options' header on the Cleanup page. Warns the user not to enable categories whose consequences they do not understand, and points at the dry-run option. Keep the warning exactly as blunt as the English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Deleted" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminado</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a run that deleted files permanently. It must read as unrecoverable, unlike the 'Moved' heading used for Recycle Bin runs. Narrow column (about 90 px).</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_EstimatedSize" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tamaño est.</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a dry run. 'Est.' is short for 'estimated'; the column is about 90 px wide, so keep it short.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Item" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Elemento</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the names of the cleaned-up suggestions.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Moved" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Movido</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item sizes, after a run that sent files to the Recycle Bin. It must not read as if the files were destroyed. Narrow column (about 90 px).</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_Column_Status" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Estado</value>
    <comment>[header] Column heading in the cleanup result dialog, over the per-item outcome (succeeded, partial, failed, skipped). Column is about 100 px wide.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_PreviewFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de simulación — Error en la vista previa</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a dry run that produced no results at all. Nothing was deleted and deletion stays blocked.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_PreviewIncomplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de simulación — Vista previa incompleta</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a dry run that partly failed. Deletion stays blocked until the preview is clean.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_DryRun_ReadyForReview" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de simulación — Listo para revisar la eliminación</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a clean dry run. This is the one state in which the dialog also offers the two delete buttons, so it must not sound like anything has been deleted yet.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_NoOutcome" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de limpieza — Sin resultado confirmado</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a real run that reported nothing back, so it is unknown what was removed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_OneTargetFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Error en 1 ruta: {0}</value>
    <comment>[safety] Small red note under an item in the cleanup result dialog when exactly one path could not be processed. {0} is that file or folder path.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_PartialOrFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de limpieza — Resultado parcial o con errores</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a real run in which some items failed or were skipped.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_PermanentComplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de limpieza — Eliminación definitiva completada</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a successful run that deleted the files permanently. The user must be able to tell from the title alone that the files are gone, unlike the Recycle Bin title.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_RecycleBinComplete" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Informe de limpieza — Movimiento a la Papelera de reciclaje completado</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the cleanup result dialog after a successful run that moved the files to the Recycle Bin. The user must be able to tell from the title alone that the files are still recoverable, unlike the permanent-deletion title.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Report_TargetsFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Error en {0} rutas; la primera: {1}</value>
    <comment>[safety] Small red note under an item in the cleanup result dialog when several paths could not be processed. {0} is how many failed, {1} is the first failing path.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ReviewNotice_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>El análisis es de solo lectura. Al ejecutar, la Papelera de reciclaje sigue siendo la vía recomendada y la simulación permite obtener una vista previa exacta de las categorías seleccionadas.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the information banner at the top of the Cleanup page. Explains that analysing changes nothing, that the Recycle Bin is the recommended (recoverable) deletion path, and that a dry run can preview the result first. Do not shorten or soften the Recycle Bin wording.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_ReviewNotice_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Revisar antes de eliminar</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the standing information banner at the top of the Cleanup page, above the options. Reminds the user to check the list before deleting.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Counts" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>({0} correctos, {1} parciales, {2} con errores, {3} omitidos)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Bracketed counts appended to the cleanup summary sentence on the Cleanup page, after a single space and followed by a full stop. {0} fully succeeded, {1} partly succeeded, {2} failed, {3} were skipped.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Se eliminaron definitivamente {0} de datos lógicos de archivos; el espacio en disco realmente recuperado puede diferir</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a run that deleted permanently. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. The counts sentence and a full stop are appended after it, so this text ends without punctuation. The user must be able to tell this apart from the Recycle Bin summary.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_Preview" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>La vista previa procesó aproximadamente {0} de datos lógicos de archivos ({1} correctos, {2} parciales, {3} con errores, {4} omitidos).</value>
    <comment>[safety] Summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a dry run. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'; {1}-{4} are counts of items that fully succeeded, partly succeeded, failed and were skipped. 'Logical file data' is the size the files report, not the space actually reclaimed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Se movieron {0} a la Papelera de reciclaje; el espacio en disco seguirá ocupado hasta que se vacíe</value>
    <comment>[safety] First half of the summary sentence shown on the Cleanup page and at the top of the result dialog after a run that used the Recycle Bin. {0} is a formatted size such as '1.2 GB'. The counts sentence and a full stop are appended after it, so this text ends without punctuation. The user must be able to tell this apart from the permanent-deletion summary.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_Summary_ResolveFailures" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Resuelva los errores de la vista previa antes de que se habilite la eliminación.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Sentence appended to the dry-run summary on the Cleanup page when the preview was not clean. It tells the user deletion stays blocked until the preview succeeds, so it must not sound optional.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Cleanup_UseRecycleBin_Header" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enviar a la Papelera de reciclaje (recomendado)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Label of the toggle switch on the Cleanup page that chooses the recoverable deletion path. On = files go to the Windows Recycle Bin; off = they are deleted permanently. This is the single control that decides recoverable vs. unrecoverable, so it must never read as if it could mean permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ClearDownloads_Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Elimina TODO el contenido de {0}. Esto incluye documentos, archivos comprimidos, elementos multimedia y cualquier otro archivo que se haya descargado. Ahorro estimado: {1}. Esta acción no se puede deshacer fácilmente: utilice el modo Papelera de reciclaje.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Warning paragraph under the 'clear entire Downloads folder' suggestion — the last thing the user reads before selecting a destructive action. {0} is the absolute Downloads path, {1} is an already-formatted size. 'ALL' is capitalised deliberately for emphasis; keep the emphasis and the undo warning at full strength. 'Recycle Bin mode' is the app's deletion mode that sends files to the Windows Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_ClearDownloads_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Vaciar toda la carpeta Descargas ({0} archivos)</value>
    <comment>[safety] Headline of the opt-in destructive cleanup suggestion that removes everything from the Downloads folder, not only installers. Appears in the Cleanup page list next to a risk badge. {0} is an already-grouped file count such as '3,417'. Do not soften 'entire'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Database_CompactFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No se pudo compactar la base de datos. No se eliminó ningún dato de análisis.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Error banner after a failed attempt to rebuild StorageMaster's own database file. The second sentence reassures the user that no stored scan data was lost — keep that reassurance explicit. The technical detail is appended after this text in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>«{0}» no se movió a la Papelera de reciclaje.</value>
    <comment>[safety] First line of the error dialog shown when moving a file to the Recycle Bin failed. {0} is the file name. The reason follows on its own line and is either the technical text from the deletion service (English) or Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Fallback. Must stay unambiguous that nothing was deleted.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Fallback" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Puede que el archivo esté bloqueado o que se haya denegado el acceso.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Reason line in the failed-deletion dialog, used when the deletion service gave no reason of its own. 'locked' means another program is holding the file open.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Error_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No se pudo eliminar el archivo</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the error dialog shown when moving a file to the Recycle Bin from the Results page failed. The file is still on disk.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteFile_Warning_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Archivo movido con una advertencia de registro interno</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of a dialog shown after a file was successfully moved to the Recycle Bin but the app could not fully update its own records of it. The file itself is fine; only the app's bookkeeping is incomplete. The detail below comes from the deletion service and stays English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScanFromHistory" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar este análisis del historial</value>
    <comment>[safety] Tooltip and screen-reader name of the trash-can button in the Results page header. Removes this scan's stored record from the app's history; it does not delete any file on disk. Keep that distinction clear so nobody reads it as deleting the scanned files.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScan_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>¿Quitar definitivamente del historial el análisis de «{0}» ({1})?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the confirmation dialog for removing a scan from the app's history. {0} is the scanned folder or drive path, {1} the scan's date. The sentence 'This cannot be undone.' (Safety_CannotBeUndone) is appended on its own line after this text, so do not repeat it here. Do not soften 'permanently'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_DeleteScan_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>¿Eliminar este análisis?</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the confirmation dialog shown before a scan record is removed from the app's history. No user file is deleted by this action, only the stored scan.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar mediante {0}</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Label of the confirming button in that dialog - the one that actually deletes. {0} is the deletion route (Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine / _RecycleBin / _Permanent), so the button reads for example 'Delete via Recycle Bin' or 'Delete via permanent deletion'. Naming the route on the button itself is deliberate: it is the user's last chance to notice that deletion is permanent.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>¿Eliminar {0} archivo(s) duplicado(s) de la página {1}?

En cada grupo se conserva intacto un archivo. Método: {2}.</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Body of the confirmation dialog shown before duplicates are deleted. {0} is an already-formatted count of files, {1} the page number of the group list, {2} the deletion route (Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine / _RecycleBin / _Permanent). The blank line between the question and the reassurance must be kept. The second sentence promises that exactly one file in every group survives - do not weaken or drop it, and never make this dialog shorter or friendlier than the English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar los duplicados seleccionados de la página actual</value>
    <comment>[dialog] Title of the confirmation dialog shown before any duplicate is deleted. Says plainly what is about to happen and that it is limited to the page of groups currently on screen. Its body is Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Modo de eliminación: eliminación definitiva.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown when neither quarantine nor the Recycle Bin is chosen. This is the unrecoverable route - the wording must be unmistakably stronger than the Recycle Bin one, using the target language's Windows term for permanent deletion.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_Quarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Modo de eliminación: cuarentena. Los archivos se pueden restaurar más abajo.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown while quarantine is chosen. Tells the user deleted duplicates are moved to quarantine and can be put back from the list further down the same page. Never drop the second sentence.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteMode_RecycleBin" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Modo de eliminación: Papelera de reciclaje.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Helper line under the delete-mode switches in Advanced options, shown while the Recycle Bin is chosen: deleted duplicates go to the Windows Recycle Bin and can be recovered from there. Use the exact Windows term for the Recycle Bin.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeleteSelected" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminar lo seleccionado</value>
    <comment>[safety] Primary destructive toolbar button on the Duplicates page. Deletes every file ticked on the current page of duplicate groups, using the delete mode chosen in Advanced options (quarantine, Recycle Bin or permanent). A confirmation dialog always follows; this label must read as an action, not a question.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_DeletingProgress" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Eliminando duplicados… {0} de {1} grupo(s) procesado(s).</value>
    <comment>[status] Status line at the bottom of the Duplicates page while a deletion is running. {0} is the number of groups already handled and {1} the total, both already formatted. Ends with a horizontal-ellipsis character in English.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Las coincidencias exactas se pueden seleccionar por lotes. Las coincidencias aproximadas de texto, imagen y vídeo requieren revisión; la cuarentena sigue siendo la vía preferida cuando existen metadatos de restauración.</value>
    <comment>[safety] Body of the safety banner at the top of the Duplicates page. Three claims, all of which must survive translation intact: (1) exact (byte-identical) matches are safe to select in bulk; (2) approximate ('fuzzy') matches of text documents, images and video are NOT, and each must be looked at by the user before deletion; (3) quarantine - a reversible move to a holding area that records where the file came from - is the preferred deletion route whenever a restore record can be written. Never shorten or soften this: it is what stops a user deleting files that only look alike.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>La seguridad es lo primero</value>
    <comment>[safety] Title of the blue information banner pinned at the top of the Duplicates page, above everything else. Introduces Safety_Duplicates_InfoBar_Message, which explains which matches may be bulk-selected for deletion and which must be reviewed one by one. Short headline; do not weaken it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Method_Permanent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>eliminación definitiva</value>
    <comment>[safety] Name of the deletion route, used inside two longer sentences on the Duplicates page: as {2} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Body and as {0} of Safety_Duplicates_ConfirmDelete_Action ('Delete via permanent deletion'). This is the unrecoverable route and must read as such - never as a plain word for 'delete'.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Safety_Duplicates_Method_Quarantine" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>cuarentena (restaurable)</value>
